1. cut – Extract columns, characters and fields
Syntax:
cut [OPTIONS] filename


Example file (creds.txt):
admin;password123;admin@example.com
john;secret456;john@example.com
alice;qwerty789;alice@example.com
root;toor;root@example.com


Field extraction
Command	Explanation
cut -d';' -f1 creds.txt	Extract first field
cut -d';' -f2 creds.txt	Extract second field
cut -d';' -f3 creds.txt	Extract third field
cut -d';' -f1,3 creds.txt	Extract fields 1 and 3
cut -d';' -f1-2 creds.txt	Extract fields 1 to 2
cut -d';' -f2- creds.txt	Extract field 2 onwards
cut -d';' -f-2 creds.txt	Extract first 2 fields

Character slicing
cut -c1 file.txt       # First character
cut -c1-5 file.txt     # Characters 1–5
cut -c3-8 file.txt     # Characters 3–8
cut -c5- file.txt      # Character 5 onwards
cut -c-4 file.txt      # First 4 characters
cut -c1,3,5 file.txt   # Characters 1, 3, 5


Byte slicing
cut -b1-5 file.txt
cut -b3-8 file.txt
cut -b5- file.txt


Useful when examining binary-like text or fixed-width ASCII data.
Custom delimiter examples
cut -d':' -f1 /etc/passwd
cut -d':' -f7 /etc/passwd
cut -d' ' -f1 file.txt
cut -d'|' -f2 file.txt
cut -d',' -f1 file.csv
cut -d'=' -f2 file.txt


CTF example: Extract usernames and passwords
cut -d';' -f1 creds.txt > users.txt
cut -d';' -f2 creds.txt > passwords.txt



2. awk – Advanced column extraction and slicing
awk is one of the most useful tools for CTF text processing.

Syntax:
awk 'PATTERN { ACTION }' file.txt


Column extraction
awk '{print $1}' file.txt
awk '{print $2}' file.txt
awk '{print $1,$3}' file.txt
awk '{print $NF}' file.txt
awk '{print $(NF-1)}' file.txt


Custom delimiters
awk -F';' '{print $1}' creds.txt
awk -F';' '{print $2}' creds.txt
awk -F':' '{print $1}' /etc/passwd
awk -F',' '{print $2}' data.csv
awk -F'|' '{print $1,$3}' data.txt


Character slicing with substr()
awk '{print substr($0,1,5)}' file.txt
awk '{print substr($0,3,8)}' file.txt
awk '{print substr($0,5)}' file.txt


Syntax:
substr(string, starting_position, length)


Conditional extraction
awk -F';' '$2=="admin" {print $1}' creds.txt
awk 'length($0)>10' file.txt
awk 'length($0)<5' file.txt
awk 'NR==5 {print}' file.txt
awk 'NR>=5 && NR<=10' file.txt


Extract unique usernames
awk -F';' '!seen[$1]++ {print $1}' creds.txt



3. sed – Delete, replace and truncate text
sed is useful for modifying text without opening an editor.
Remove characters
sed 's/hello//' file.txt
sed 's/^...//' file.txt
sed 's/.....$//' file.txt


Explanation:
- ^ = Beginning of line.
- $ = End of line.
- . = Any single character.
- ... = Three characters.
Replace delimiters
sed 's/;/ /g' file.txt
sed 's/:/;/g' file.txt
sed 's/,/;/g' file.txt


Extract specific lines
sed -n '5p' file.txt
sed -n '5,10p' file.txt
sed -n '1,20p' file.txt


Delete lines
sed '1d' file.txt
sed '5d' file.txt
sed '1,5d' file.txt
sed '/admin/d' file.txt
sed '/^$/d' file.txt


Remove prefixes and suffixes
sed 's/^.*;//' file.txt
sed 's/;.*$//' file.txt



4. grep – Search and filter text
Basic searches
grep "admin" file.txt
grep -i "admin" file.txt
grep -v "admin" file.txt
grep -n "admin" file.txt
grep -c "admin" file.txt


Option	Purpose
-i	Ignore case
-v	Invert match
-n	Show line number
-c	Count matches
-o	Print only matching text
-E	Extended regex
-r	Recursive search
-w	Match whole word

Regex extraction
grep -oE '[0-9]+' file.txt
grep -oE '[a-zA-Z]+' file.txt
grep -oE '[a-zA-Z0-9]+' file.txt
grep -oE '[0-9]{4,}' file.txt
grep -oE '[^;]+' file.txt


Find interesting CTF strings
grep -iE 'flag|password|secret|token|admin' file.txt
grep -iE 'cyber|ctf|htb|thm' file.txt
grep -E '^[0-9]+' file.txt
grep -E '^[a-zA-Z0-9_]+$' file.txt



5. tr – Translate, delete and squeeze characters
Character transformations
tr 'a-z' 'A-Z' < file.txt
tr 'A-Z' 'a-z' < file.txt
tr '0-9' 'X' < file.txt


Delete characters
tr -d ' ' < file.txt
tr -d '\n' < file.txt
tr -d '\r' < file.txt
tr -d '[:punct:]' < file.txt
tr -d '[:digit:]' < file.txt


Remove or normalize whitespace
tr -s ' ' < file.txt
tr -s '\n' < file.txt
tr '\t' ' ' < file.txt


Reverse alphabet (ROT13)
tr 'A-Za-z' 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m' < file.txt



6. head and tail – Extract lines
head file.txt
head -n 5 file.txt
head -n 20 file.txt
tail file.txt
tail -n 5 file.txt
tail -n 20 file.txt
tail -f access.log


Remove first or last lines
tail -n +6 file.txt
head -n -5 file.txt


- tail -n +6 = Start from line 6.
- head -n -5 = Remove last 5 lines.

7. sort and uniq – Wordlist processing
Very useful for password lists and username enumeration.
sort file.txt
sort -r file.txt
sort -u file.txt
sort -n file.txt
sort -f file.txt


Duplicate detection
uniq file.txt
uniq -c file.txt
uniq -d file.txt
uniq -u file.txt


Remember: uniq detects adjacent duplicates, so sort first.
sort file.txt | uniq
sort file.txt | uniq -c
sort file.txt | uniq -d


Remove duplicates and save
sort -u passwords.txt > clean_passwords.txt



8. paste and join – Combine text files
Combine side-by-side
paste users.txt passwords.txt
paste -d';' users.txt passwords.txt


Combine files line-by-line
paste -s file.txt


Join matching records
join users.txt passwords.txt


For join, both files should be sorted by their matching key.

9. rev – Reverse text
rev file.txt
echo "admin" | rev


Example:
Input:  cyberstrike
Output: ekirtsrebyc


Useful for reversing strings in basic encoding challenges.

10. fold – Split long lines
fold -w 10 file.txt
fold -w 5 file.txt
fold -s -w 20 file.txt


- -w = Maximum width.
- -s = Break at spaces where possible.
Useful for long Base64 strings and encoded challenge output.

🧠 CTF Practical Recipes
These are combinations you'll frequently use during challenges.
1. Extract usernames from credential dumps
cut -d';' -f1 creds-dump.txt > users.txt


2. Extract passwords
cut -d';' -f2 creds-dump.txt > passwords.txt


3. Extract usernames and remove duplicates
cut -d';' -f1 creds-dump.txt | sort -u > users.txt


4. Extract passwords longer than 8 characters
awk 'length($0)>8' passwords.txt


5. Extract emails
grep -oE '[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\.[A-Za-z]{2,}' file.txt


6. Extract IP addresses
grep -oE '([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}' file.txt


This matches IP-like strings; it does not validate whether each address is a valid IPv4 address.
7. Extract hexadecimal strings
grep -oE '0x[0-9a-fA-F]+' file.txt


8. Remove blank lines
sed '/^$/d' file.txt


9. Remove Windows carriage returns
tr -d '\r' < file.txt > clean.txt


10. Extract text after semicolon
sed 's/^.*;//' file.txt


11. Extract text before semicolon
sed 's/;.*$//' file.txt


12. Get first 8 characters from every line
cut -c1-8 file.txt


13. Get last 5 characters from every line
awk '{print substr($0,length($0)-4)}' file.txt


14. Count unique lines
sort -u file.txt | wc -l


15. Find longest lines
awk '{print length($0),$0}' file.txt | sort -rn | head



⚡ Quick Command Reference
Task	Command
First column	cut -d';' -f1 file
Last column	awk -F';' '{print $NF}' file
First 5 chars	cut -c1-5 file
Last 5 chars	rev file \| cut -c1-5 \| rev
Remove first 3 chars	cut -c4- file
Remove last 3 chars	sed 's/...$//' file
Before delimiter	cut -d';' -f1 file
After delimiter	cut -d';' -f2- file
Remove blank lines	grep -v '^$' file
Remove duplicates	sort -u file
Reverse lines	rev file
Uppercase	tr 'a-z' 'A-Z'
Lowercase	tr 'A-Z' 'a-z'
Count lines	wc -l file
Count words	wc -w file
Count characters	wc -m file
Count bytes	wc -c file
